shape theory.coherence.three-geometries : theory.coherence.law1, theory.coherence.law2, theory.coherence.law3 {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Three Laws, Three Geometries (Section 5.7).
// The Laws of Coherence produce three distinct geometric constraints
// on any shape system:
//
// Law 2 (Conservation) -> internal geometry:
//   The mixing angle triangle. Source and destination are independent
//   components of a conserved persistence magnitude.
//   p^2 = s_theta^2 + c_theta^2
//   This is the geometry of a single transformation.
//
// Law 1 (Stable Reference) -> relational geometry:
//   How shapes in contact maintain coherent reference. When shapes
//   couple at a junction, Law 1 requires joint coherence across both
//   sides, producing conservation stacking: sin^2(theta) = c/p.
//   This is the geometry of coupling and emergence.
//
// Law 3 (Consistency) -> resolution geometry:
//   The resolution triangle that determines where the outcome sits
//   in the space of all structural positions after incompatible
//   shapes interact. This is the geometry of forcing, collapse, and
//   outcome determination.
//
// The three laws are not separate theories applied in sequence.
// They are three aspects of a single coherence requirement,
// operating simultaneously at every level.
//
// Derives from: all three relational laws
  """
}
